   \"evidence\": \"A5's fear of 'seating everyone before we're protected' makes ordinary party furniture the concrete consequence of A1's faith in safeguards.\"\n          }\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 89\n      },\n      \"hardGates\": {\n        \"fiveSpokenTurns\": true,\n        \"aHoldsRequestedCard\": true,\n        \"b2CausesA3\": true,\n        \"b4CausesA5\": true,\n        \"noSeparateNarration\": true,\n        \"noUnrelatedPunchline\": true,\n        \"noRealPartnerInstruction\": true,\n        \"noForcedEnding\": true\n      },\n      \"hardGateEvidence\": {\n        \"b2CausesA3\": \"B2 says the first two committees lost the application; A3 interprets the third committee as a reassuring backup.\",\n        \"b4CausesA5\": \"B4 says a fresh application is required and the tables cannot remain reserved; A5 chooses reapplication and releasing the tables to preserve approval-first ordering.\",\n        \"cardContribution\": \"A1 praises review, A3 celebrates institutional redundancy, and A5 treats formal approval as protection against the chaos of premature seating.\",\n        \"sameGameHeightened\": \"A5 moves from defending delays verbally to accepting the loss of a useful booking. Its instruction concerns fictional organizing work and leaves B free to respond.\"\n      },\n      \"reservation\": \"A3's 'One lost envelope' understates B2's report that both earlier committees lost the application. It can play as sincere minimization, but is a small continuity blemish. No rewrite needed.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"The permit alternative accommodates B through an online application. This version makes A accept a concrete organizing setback, giving the same institutional optimism a more consequential tactic.\",\n      \"accept\": true\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S190-single-a\",\n      \"candidateHash\": \"8b2ec502189dc2a65d9bf3e435b44c9a89adbd86d2d93a46956feeed6b608e21\",\n      \"secondPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n